{
  "decision": "ROLLBACK",
  "why": "Evidence: no availability signal at all - 0 5xx on both arms, every endpoint, all four 30s windows, and 0.00% err%. Aggregate latency is also fine at the head: canary p50 33ms vs stable 35ms, canary p95 95ms vs stable 95ms. The one real signal is the tail: canary aggregate p99 172ms vs stable 104ms (+65%). Per-route it is isolated, not global: /browse (canary p50/p95/p99 22/27/30 vs stable 22/27/28) and /checkout (79/102/105 vs 82/103/106) are at parity or slightly better, so the whole +65% comes from /search, where canary p50 47ms and p95 60ms are IDENTICAL to stable (47ms/59ms) but p99 is 652ms vs stable 61ms - a >10x tail blowup. Stable /search is extremely tight over 2273 requests (p95 59 -> p99 61, a 2ms spread), so a bimodal tail is new behavior in 1.1 rather than something inherent to the route. The over-time table shows canary p95 92-95 vs stable 93-97 with no drift and 0 5xx in every window, so this is not a leak or saturation trend that grew during the test. Judgement: p50/p95 parity combined with a 10x p99 spike on a single endpoint is the signature of a rare slow path (cold-cache miss, lock contention, or an N+1 query on some search inputs) rather than broad slowness. Promoting moves /search from ~25% to 100% of traffic - roughly 4x the volume through that path with 1/4 the current pod headroom - and contention-style causes amplify rather than scale linearly, so the observed 652ms is a floor, not a ceiling, for post-promote impact. Cost asymmetry supports holding: a rollback costs a delayed release, while a bad promote ships an unbounded search tail to all customers. Caveat stated honestly: the canary p99 is estimated from only ~7 of 729 /search requests, so its magnitude is noisy and partly attributable to the single canary pod (possible JIT/cache warmup). However the direction is not explainable by sampling error alone - canary's 1%-worst mark sits at 652ms while stable's sits at 61ms across 3x the sample. Recommended next step: do not promote on this data; reproduce /search at canary-equivalent or higher RPS with p99 and max latency plus traces on the slow requests, confirm the slow path and fix or bound it, then re-run the canary. If the tail proves to be pod warmup only, this becomes a safe promote on the re-run at near-zero cost."
}

{
  "decision": "ROLLBACK",
  "why": "EVIDENCE - what is clean: availability is perfect on both sides (canary 0 5xx / 2384 reqs, stable 0 5xx / 7404 reqs, 0.00% err% on every endpoint and in all four 30s windows). Central latency is clean too: canary vs stable p50 33 vs 35ms, p95 95 vs 95ms overall; /browse 22/27 vs 22/27; /search p50 47 vs 47 and p95 60 vs 59; /checkout is actually BETTER on the canary (p50 79 vs 82, p95 102 vs 103, p99 105 vs 106). The over-time table shows no drift or degradation across 0-30 / 30-60 / 60-90 / 90-120s. EVIDENCE - the one bad signal: tail latency on /search. Canary /search p99 = 652ms vs stable 61ms (>10x), and canary overall p99 = 172ms vs stable 104ms. INFERENCE (judgement, derived from the table): those two numbers corroborate each other and size the blast radius. Canary overall p99 = 172ms means ~1% of 2384 requests, roughly 24 requests, were at or above 172ms. Canary /browse p99 is 30ms and /checkout p99 is 105ms, so essentially none of those 24 can come from browse or checkout - they must be /search. That puts ~24 of 729 canary search requests (~3%) in a 170ms-to-650ms+ band, while stable /search goes p95 59 -> p99 61ms and stable overall p99 is only 104ms, i.e. stable produces essentially nothing above ~110ms. So the canary did not just get noisier, it grew a second slow mode on /search that stable does not have. JUDGEMENT - why this is not noise: a p99 from 729 samples is individually low-confidence, but this is not one flaky datapoint - the magnitude is 10x, it is far outside anything stable's very tight distribution produces, and it is independently visible in the aggregate p99 row. The flat p95 over time does not clear it: the over-time table has no p99 column, and p95 cannot see a 3% tail. Error rate cannot see it either, because these requests are slow, not failing. JUDGEMENT - the call: promoting puts 4x this traffic onto version 1.1 with an unexplained multi-hundred-ms tail on a user-facing path, and per-pod tail latency typically degrades, not improves, at higher load - so the promoted blast radius is at least ~3% of all search traffic. Given the stated asymmetry that a bad promote hurts customers, I will not promote on this data. ACTION: roll back to version 1.0 (no customer impact so far - zero errors, so this is a precautionary hold, not an incident), pull traces for the canary's slow /search requests (suspect an uncached or unindexed query branch hit by a minority of search terms), fix or explain the second mode, then re-run the canary. Promote once canary /search p99 and canary overall p99 match stable."
}

{
  "decision": "ROLLBACK",
  "why": "EVIDENCE (dashboard D, 120s / 80 req/s / 9788 reqs). What is clean: availability is perfect on both sides - 0 5xx for stable (7404 reqs) and 0 5xx for canary (2384 reqs), in every one of the four 30s windows. Body-of-distribution latency is equal or better on the canary for every endpoint: /browse p50/p95 22/27 vs stable 22/27, /search 47/60 vs 47/59, /checkout 79/102 vs stable 82/103 (the money path is slightly faster). Per-window canary p95 is flat (95, 95, 92, 95) against stable (96, 93, 94, 97), so no drift, warm-up trend or crash loop. Per-pod load is comparable, so the canary is not under-exercised: 2384 reqs on 1 canary pod vs 7404/3 = ~2468 per stable pod. THE ONE DIVERGENCE: canary /search p99 = 652ms vs stable 61ms (~10.7x), and that single endpoint lifts canary overall p99 to 172ms vs stable 104ms. /search p50 and p95 are identical to stable, so the regression lives entirely in the top few percent of requests - a bimodal slow path, not a general slowdown. WHY I DO NOT WRITE IT OFF AS SINGLE-POD NOISE (judgement, from arithmetic on the given numbers): the standard benign explanation for a tail spike on a 1-pod canary is a process-wide stall (GC pause, cold start, CPU steal), and a 3-pod stable pool does dilute per-pod tails. But a process-wide stall slows every endpoint on that pod. To get /search p99 = 652ms with 729 requests, roughly the 7 slowest requests must be >=652ms, i.e. about a second or more of blocked time on that pod. On the same pod in the same window, /browse (1190 reqs) has p99 = 30ms and /checkout (465 reqs) has p99 = 105ms - both clean. A stall of that size would have pushed /browse's ~12-request p99 far above 30ms. So the most consistent reading is a /search-code-path-specific slow branch in 1.1 affecting roughly 1-4% of searches at ~10x latency, not a shared-pause artifact. RISK OF PROMOTING: promotion takes /search from ~25% to 100% of traffic, about 4x volume onto that path. Judgement: a bimodal slow path (cache miss, unindexed query, retry/timeout branch) typically degrades with volume rather than improving, and 650ms can become timeouts and 5xx once connection pools and upstream budgets are involved. The dashboard reports p95 per window but not p99 per window, so I cannot even tell whether the tail was a one-off cold-cache burst at the start or steady - the data needed to clear the canary was not captured. DECISION LOGIC: given the stated cost asymmetry (a bad promote hurts customers, a bad rollback only blocks a release), the cheap reversible move is to not ship an unexplained 10x tail regression on a user-facing endpoint. This is a hold-pending-evidence rollback, not a claim that 1.1 is broken - availability and checkout latency are genuinely healthy. WHAT WOULD FLIP ME TO PROMOTE: re-run the canary at 2-3 pods (so per-pod tail statistics are comparable to stable) with >5k /search requests, reporting p99 and p99.9 per 30s window and per pod, plus upstream/DB timings for /search. If /search p99 stays within ~1.5x of stable and the tail is not front-loaded, promote immediately - this is a tail-latency question only."
}

{
  "decision": "PROMOTE",
  "why": "EVIDENCE (dashboard D: 120 s, 80 req/s, 9788 reqs; stable v1.0 x3 pods = 7404 reqs, canary v1.1 x1 pod = 2384 reqs, ~24% of traffic). Availability: canary 0 5xx / 2384 reqs = 0.00%, same as stable (0 / 7404). Central latency: canary p50 33 ms vs stable 35 ms, canary p95 95 ms vs stable 95 ms - no regression. Per endpoint the canary matches or beats stable on p50/p95: /browse 22/27 vs 22/27, /search 47/60 vs 47/59, /checkout 79/102 vs 82/103 (checkout, the revenue path, is marginally faster). Stability over time: canary p95 across the four 30 s windows is 95, 95, 92, 95 ms vs stable 96, 93, 94, 97 ms, and 5xx stays 0 in every window - no drift, no warm-up cliff, no late degradation. ONLY ANOMALY: tail latency. Canary overall p99 172 ms vs stable 104 ms, driven entirely by /search p99 652 ms vs stable 61 ms. JUDGEMENT on that anomaly: I treat it as a non-blocking tail artifact rather than a real regression, because (a) p99 over 729 canary /search requests is set by roughly the 7 slowest requests, so it is a very low-confidence statistic, (b) canary /search p50 and p95 are identical to stable (47 / 60 vs 47 / 59), which is inconsistent with a systemic slowdown and consistent with a few isolated slow requests such as single-pod cold start or cache/connection warm-up, and (c) none of those slow requests failed - error rate is still 0%. Blast radius: ~1% of /search on the canary is ~0.07% of total traffic, slow but successful, versus blocking a release on roughly 7 requests. LIMITS OF THE EVIDENCE (judgement): with 0/2384 observed failures the rule of three puts the 95% upper bound on canary error rate at ~0.13%, so a very small error rate cannot be excluded; there is also no CPU/memory/saturation data, no per-pod breakdown, only 120 s of observation, and the canary served ~25% of traffic as a single pod, so this does not prove behaviour at 4x the load. DECISION: promote, with conditions - ramp rather than jumping straight to 100% if the tooling allows, and watch /search p99 plus overall 5xx for the first several minutes at full traffic; roll back if /search p99 stays above ~200 ms once all pods are warm, or if 5xx exceeds 0.1% on any endpoint."
}
